<h2>The three statutory sources</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 144 BNSS.</strong> The secular, summary route before a Magistrate: parents unable to maintain themselves, child with sufficient means, neglect or refusal — the three ingredients. Gender-neutral by text.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 20(3) HAMA.</strong> For Hindus, a personal-law obligation to maintain aged or infirm parents (including a childless stepmother) so far as they cannot maintain themselves from their own resources — enforceable by civil suit, with quantum guided by Section 23.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Senior Citizens Act, 2007.</strong> The Tribunal route: expansive definitions, a ninety-day disposal discipline, monthly maintenance orders, and enforcement mechanisms including the power to treat default seriously. The 2007 Act also carries Section 23, allowing cancellation of property transfers made on the unfulfilled condition of being maintained.</div>
</div>

<h2>What &ldquo;unable to maintain themselves&rdquo; means for parents</h2>
<p>The parent&rsquo;s inability is assessed realistically, not theoretically. A small pension or rental income does not disqualify a parent whose medical and living costs exceed it; conversely, parents holding substantial income-yielding assets will struggle to show inability. Counsel for parents should file a candid statement of income, expenses and health costs — credibility at this threshold decides the case&rsquo;s temperature. Counsel for the daughter should obtain discovery of the parents&rsquo; actual holdings, including property transferred to other siblings, which is often the real grievance driving the petition.</p>

<h2>The daughter&rsquo;s means: the real battleground</h2>
<p>Because liability follows means, the contest in daughter cases is almost always evidentiary. Three propositions frame it. First, only the daughter&rsquo;s own income and assets count; a Magistrate cannot indirectly charge the son-in-law. Second, &ldquo;means&rdquo; is not limited to salary — earning capacity, professional qualifications and property all enter the assessment, as they do for sons. Third, the daughter&rsquo;s own obligations — her children&rsquo;s education, dependent in-laws, EMIs — are relevant to quantum, not to liability. The order that emerges is typically a modest monthly figure calibrated to leave her household functional.</p>

<h2>Defences that work, and ones that do not</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Defence</th><th>Prospects</th></tr>
  <tr><td>&ldquo;I have no independent income&rdquo;</td><td>Good, if true and proved — liability needs means</td></tr>
  <tr><td>&ldquo;My siblings should share&rdquo;</td><td>Good for apportionment; no answer to liability itself</td></tr>
  <tr><td>&ldquo;The parents gave all property to my brother&rdquo;</td><td>Relevant equity; courts may direct the propertied sibling to bear more, and the parents&rsquo; Section 23 (2007 Act) remedy against that transfer may be the better route</td></tr>
  <tr><td>&ldquo;I am married and belong to another family&rdquo;</td><td>Fails — marriage does not dissolve the parent-child obligation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>&ldquo;The parents treated me badly&rdquo;</td><td>Rarely succeeds against genuine destitution; the remedy is calibrated quantum, not denial</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Choosing the forum</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Urgent subsistence →</strong> Section 144 BNSS: interim maintenance is available and the Magistrate&rsquo;s process is familiar.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Elderly parents, multiple children, property friction →</strong> Senior Citizens Tribunal: summary timeline, apportionment among children, and the transfer-cancellation power under Section 23.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Estate-linked or complex Hindu family claims →</strong> HAMA civil suit, where fuller adjudication and charge-creation on property are available.</div>
</div>
